  "final_answer": "I have successfully retrieved the answer from WolframAlpha. The result is clearly displayed in the DOM snapshot:\n\n**General Solution:**\n\n$$y(t) = e^t \\cdot c_1 \\sin(3t) + e^t \\cdot c_2 \\cos(3t)$$\n\nOr equivalently:\n$$y(t) = e^t \\left(c_1 \\sin(3t) + c_2 \\cos(3t)\\right)$$\n\n**Verification of the solution:**\n\nFor the differential equation $y''(t) - 2y'(t) + 10y(t) = 0$, the characteristic equation is:\n$$r^2 - 2r + 10 = 0$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\n$$r = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 40}}{2} = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} = \\frac{2 \\pm 6i}{2} = 1 \\pm 3i$$\n\nSince the roots are complex with real part 1 and imaginary part ±3, the general solution is:\n$$y(t) = e^t \\left(c_1 \\cos(3t) + c_2 \\sin(3t)\\right)$$\n\nWolframAlpha confirmed this answer. The website also noted additional related information, including that this is an autonomous ODE and a Sturm-Liouville equation.",
